  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/01/X-Men_Vol_1_3.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "While practicing, Professor X calls Cyclops \"Scott\" (the first time, he was referred to previously only as \"Slim\") and says he's probably the most powerful X-Man but also so grim.&nbsp; Scott worries about his power beam.&nbsp; Beast, Iceman and Angel clown around before the professor calls for Jean's turn at practice.&nbsp; But it is interrupted when the professor senses another mutant and sends the team off to investigate.&nbsp; They all fight over who gets to team up with Jean, of course, except Scott, who is too tormented by the possibility that someone might get hurt as a result of his power beams.\n\nYet in the end, Jean decides that she'd like to go with the quiet Scott Summers, much to the annoyance of Warren Worthington, who simply scoops her up, drops her in his expensive sports car, and heads out!&nbsp; Iceman admits that Angel is the \"glamor boy\" of the group.&nbsp; Hank and Bobby are very vocal about their interest in Jean, while Scott is mostly silent and serious.\n\nSent out searching in their normal clothes, the various X-Men come across a couple of false alarms before Scott heads into a carnival and encounters \"the Blob\" which Professor X confirms is the mutant they seek.&nbsp; Scott watches as people try to move the Blob to no avail.&nbsp; They even shoot him with bullets, but they just sink into his flesh until he flexes it, and they all fall out.&nbsp; Scott approaches him in his trailer later, but only Jean's eventual appearance convinces him to visit the X-Men headquarters.&nbsp; The Professor realizes that once the Blob plants himself, he literally cannot be moved.\n\nThe Professor offers him admittance to the X-Men, but the Blob considers himself better than all of them and <i>refuses</i>!&nbsp; Professor X is incensed, and because the Blob has now learned their identities, the X-Men's mentor states that he has no choice but to remove that memory from his brain!&nbsp; But the Blob evades capture (after a couple of pages of battle action) and escapes through a hatch to crawl back to the city, where he plans to re-join the carnival, all the while <i>realizing</i> the threat he poses to the team, particularly now that he knows <i>who</i> they are and <i>where</i> their headquarters is.\n\nThe Professor says they can't risk him knowing their secret location and identities.&nbsp; \"He must not talk!\"&nbsp; But the Blob <i>also</i> realizes that he's dangerous to the X-Men, and now that he's learned he's a mutant, he takes over the carnival with a plan to attack the X-Men before they come after him, which he knows they will.&nbsp; Soon, the entire carnival heads out to X-Men headquarters, and the Professor realizes he will have to wipe this memory from <i>all of them</i> now!\n\nThe entire carnival attacks, and though the X-Men fight valiantly, there are simply too many of them to handle, given their current inexperience.&nbsp; They are finally overwhelmed by all the actors and animals, and the Blob leads the way toward the Professor!&nbsp; However, the Professor helps Jean to realize that she's able to free everyone by telekinetically summoning the knife-thrower's utensils to cut everyone's ropes, and the group heads inside in time to help the professor!\n\nThe ensuing scuffle gives the Professor time to finish preparing his \"intensifier ray\" that augments his ability, and he quickly makes them all forget they've ever met the X-Men.&nbsp; They're left wondering where they are and that they better get back to the carnival before they get \"sacked.\"\n\nTheir memories wiped, the carnival folks return to their lives, and the Blob no longer even realizes that he's a mutant.&nbsp; All is well, with the ending text hinting that <i>because</i> of the Blob's mutant status, perhaps one day his memory will return.&nbsp; But if it ever does... \"the X-Men will be ready!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "It's no surprise that the third issue of the X-Men introduces yet <i>another</i> mutant, the Blob, who is depicted as selfish and ego-centric, interested only in himself and what he can get as a \"freak\" of nature.&nbsp; He seems almost <i>happy</i> to learn he's better than his fellow men.\n\nThe Blob is detected early on, perhaps being missed before due to the fact that the carnival presumably travels around the country and only just now came within range of the Professor's ability to mentally detect him.\n\n[ASSET: left | TelepathicEyes | width=12%] From an artistic standpoint, one of the highlights was the Professor's roaming \"telepathic eyes,\" which Kirby draws very much like he did Loki's eyes back in [LINK:1963:AVEN_1|AVEN #1|2026-11-13].&nbsp; It's a great visual representation of a person monitoring events from afar.&nbsp; And even though all readers understand that it's only an effect (and a <i>creepy</i> one at that), it is nevertheless quite effective, [SPAN: severity=success; text=and I like it very much.]\n\n[ASSET: right | CharacterizationOnDisplay | width=15%] It seems evident right off the bat that Stan and Jack (but <i>particularly</i> Stan, as evidenced by the dialogue) very much want these characters to stand out -- to be <i>different</i> -- from already-established characters such as the Fantastic Four.\n[BR]\n[BR] Per the announcement made in this month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> 9 ([LINK:1963:ASM_9|ASM #9|2027-03-13]), Stan admits that people were already writing in to complain that the Beast was too much like the Thing.&nbsp; He remarks that this issue \"really plays up their various personalities!\"\n\nFrom the very first page, you can see Stan's effort to inject personality into the various characters.\n[HANG0] * Scott is silent and brooding (though verbally acknowledged as the most powerful of the X-Men)\n[HANG0] * Angel is shown as the \"glamor boy\" (rich and ever-so-slightly conceited)\n[HANG0] * Bobby is young, brash, and quick to start a scuffle (think Johnny Storm brawling with the Thing)\n[HANG0] * Hank reads <i>Advanced Calculus</i> books with his foot and speaks with a <i>robust</i> vocabulary.\n[HANG0] * Jean is spoken <i>about</i> more by the others than what we see directly from her, but she clearly prefers Scott.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut eventually, it was time to actually <i>introduce</i> this issue's villain-of-the-month, which the creators finally do on page 7.&nbsp; It is quickly established that <i>he</i> is the very mutant that the Professor detected earlier (on page 3).&nbsp; Nothing seems able to hurt him or even <i>move</i> him.\n[ASSET: center | MeetTheBlob | width=67%]\n\nWhen Scott approaches him on page 8, Stan even <i>correctly</i> uses the slang term \"rube,\" which was prevalent in carnival-type circles, referring to certain tiresome customers that the various performers inevitably encountered as they travelled about the country.\n\nIt may be a small detail, but it demonstrates some attention to detail here, as if Stan <i>purposefully</i> determined to devote time to fully establishing the different personalities of the various characters, in this case, even to the <i>villain</i> of the story.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=So good job there!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | TerrificBlob | width=12%] But no sooner did the Blob learn of his mutant status, which showed him he was special (and something he did not previously understand), than the Professor decide to offer X-Men membership to him.\n[BR]\n[BR] Everyone seems <i>shocked</i> when he doesn't immediately <i>jump </i>at the opportunity!\n\n[ASSET: right | HomoSuperior | width=26%] On the contrary, the Blob loses [SPAN: severity=warning; text=no time] fully embracing the fact that he's a mutant and is even quick to pick up on the jargon, indicating on page 13 that's he's \"one of the Homo Superior\" and ready to take over the carnival, ordering the \"puny\" homo sapiens to obey his every command... [SPAN: severity=error; text=or else!]\n\nJust like the Vanisher in the previous issue ([LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17]), the Blob seems all-too-willing to accept that fact that he is special (and somehow knows the term that Magneto [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=only just coined] back back in [LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25]).&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Should Maggy be demanding royalties?)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhat follows though is essentially pages upon pages of invasion -- complete with battle action -- and shows a level of escalation that is pretty much par for the course in these early Marvel stories (and perhaps even comic books in <i>general</i>), as the scene morphs from the Blob's escape from the mansion at the bottom of page 11 to the full-on assault on that very same mansion <b>only three pages later</b> (thereby automatically qualifying the book as a finalist for this year's Spiral Award).\n\n[ASSET: left | JeanFreesEveryone | width=10%] It was good to see Jean freeing the others on page 22, using her power of telekinesis to lift off her blindfold and then cut herself loose with the carnival's knife-thrower's own tools.\n[BR]\n[BR] So while her personality wasn't as developed as the others, Stan and Jack at least give her a moment of glory near the end of the book.\n\n[ASSET: right | ProfessorToTheRescue | width=14%] The ensuing scuffle gives the professor time to finish preparing his \"intensifier ray\" that augments his ability, and he quickly makes them all forget they've ever met the X-Men.&nbsp; They're left wondering where they are and that they better get back to the carnival before they get \"sacked\" (as the Blob puts it - [SPAN: severity=info; text=is he <i>British</i> perhaps?]).\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis book still hasn't really hit its stride, but this was a confident step in the right direction, not to mention being <i>far and away </i>better than the last issue.\n\nProfessor X is perhaps a [SPAN: severity=warning; text=little too quick] to offer X-Men membership to newcomers, which shows that he, too, is learning along with his teenage charges.&nbsp; All are sort of learning on the job, and that's not a bad thing, as this <i>is</i> a school after all.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=I kind of like this aspect, in fact.]\n\nAs the last couple of issues have demonstrated, there's perhaps still a bit too much of [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Professor X swoops in at the end and resets everything,\"] but at least <i>this</i> time, he got some help from the X-Men (who seemed only <i>observers</i> in their last adventure).&nbsp; And since there were a whole lot more people involved this time around, it isn't much of a stretch to accept the fact that he needed the help of a gadget to ultimately wipe the minds of all the carnival workers, which must have been far more strenuous than what he did only to the Vanisher last time.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Again... this <i>is</i> a school, so showing <i>everyone</i> learning seems about right.]\n\nThe Blob and his all-too-quick takeover of the carnival and subsequent attack on the X-Men may have been the weakest point of the story (and gave Jack Kirby an easy excuse to draw page-consuming action sequences), but it at least portrayed him as the very thing that Professor X formed the X-Men to protect Earth against, so weak or not, [SPAN: severity=success; text=the Blob served his purpose.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=But isn't it also a dangerous precedent to suggest that the solution to every evil mutant is just one quick Professor X memory wipe away?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Definitely something to keep an eye on going forward!)]\n\nBut despite its weaker moments, the book also gets points on the <b>positive side</b> for flavoring the characters with actual personalities, something letter-writers were correct to flag, [SPAN: severity=success; text=so it's nice to see Marvel responding to reader feedback even as early as the title's third issue.]&nbsp; This is something to look forward to in subsequent issues -- seeing how their various personalities blend and ultimately take shape.\n\nIt's only issue 3, so we can only watch... and wait... to see if the book can continue to move forward -- and even to spice things up -- without getting redundant...",
  "side_note": [
    "The Announcements page of [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16] reveals that the Blob got his name from a sandwich delivery boy who, upon seeing what Lee and Kirby were looking at from the drawing said, \"It looks like a big blob.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=So they named him the Blob!]",
    "While it's a very minor thing, page 12 shows the Blob ducking down into a manhole outside the Professor's school and following the tunnels back into the city.&nbsp; But is this realistic?&nbsp; Is there <i>really</i> a \"vast network of underground cables and circuits\" running from the X-Men's mansion in Westchester County all the way back to New York City?&nbsp; Is there really even a manhole to be found just outside the mansion?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=I find this hard to believe.]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(But like I said, a <i>small</i> thing...)]"
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "[ASSET: left | SecretlyInLove | width=21%] No review of this issue would be complete without at least a <i>mention</i> of the now-infamous page 4 panel where readers learn that Professor X is secretly in love with Jean.&nbsp; This ill-advised plotline was thankfully abandoned -- and never mentioned for over three decades.&nbsp; It was considered enough of a blunder to be mentioned in the <i>Marvel No-Prize Book</i> published in the 1980s.\n[BR]\n[BR] Since this archive will likely never cover issues that far down the road, as far as it is concerned, the scene will be treated as if it <i>never</i> happened.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}